WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.960
The comparison entry point creates a
fresh global run and an hour working day

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.490
run.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.070
Both fit on the same training year.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.670
Both score the same four thousand, three
hundred and fifty seven validation hours.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.310
Record identity,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.930
target time and issue time are
checked before any error reduction.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.930
Metric definitions and the unrounded
prediction policy also stay fixed.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.410
Each run saves the model,
aligned predictions,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.670
source and dependency identities,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:33.530
frozen data identities

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.190
and completion status.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.370
The parent saves their comparison.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.770
Only the predictor changes.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.730
We do not refit with validation answers,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.890
and we do not evaluate
the reserved final test.

00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:52.745
The global mean has validation mean
absolute error of about one hundred and

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:55.735
fifty four point two
five rentals per hour.

00:00:56.305 --> 00:00:59.055
The group model has about
ninety one point five seven.

00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:02.285
Root mean squared error also falls,

00:01:02.285 --> 00:01:06.776
from about two hundred and eight point
one seven to one hundred and thirty seven

00:01:06.776 --> 00:01:07.875
point three nine.

00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:12.095
The group model captures useful
daily structure on this window.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.475
Its training errors are lower too,

00:01:14.665 --> 00:01:18.505
which is expected when separate group
constants can reproduce the global

00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:20.815
constant as one possible setting.

00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:26.135
That training squared error argument does
not guarantee a validation improvement.

00:01:26.725 --> 00:01:30.395
The later result needs measurement,
which these saved runs provide.

00:01:32.728 --> 00:01:37.878
Subtract the group model's mean absolute
error from the global model's value.

00:01:38.168 --> 00:01:42.518
The reduction is about sixty two
point six seven rentals per hour.

00:01:42.888 --> 00:01:46.038
Divide that reduction by
the original global error,

00:01:46.328 --> 00:01:47.718
then multiply by one hundred.

00:01:48.288 --> 00:01:50.598
This gives about forty
point six three percent.

00:01:51.168 --> 00:01:53.178
The denominator matters.

00:01:53.508 --> 00:01:57.358
We are reporting a relative reduction
against this reference score.

00:01:57.588 --> 00:02:00.808
It does not mean forty percent
of forecasts became exact,

00:02:00.808 --> 00:02:03.258
or that every hour improved.

00:02:03.568 --> 00:02:08.038
It is also a development comparison
for these frozen chronological periods.

00:02:08.608 --> 00:02:13.248
The reserved test and an application's
quality requirements still need their own

00:02:13.248 --> 00:02:14.218
later evidence.

00:02:16.442 --> 00:02:20.332
Consider the eleventh of January
at five in the afternoon,

00:02:20.702 --> 00:02:21.712
issued at four.

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:24.052
The recorded target is one
hundred and thirty four.

00:02:25.462 --> 00:02:29.642
The working day group predicts three
hundred and ninety four point nine two,

00:02:29.642 --> 00:02:33.752
producing an absolute error of about
two hundred and sixty point nine two.

00:02:34.462 --> 00:02:36.972
The global constant
happens to be much closer,

00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:39.352
missing by about nine point seven nine.

00:02:40.042 --> 00:02:41.312
This hour gets worse.

00:02:42.022 --> 00:02:42.752
Across validation,

00:02:43.222 --> 00:02:43.669
three thousand,

00:02:43.669 --> 00:02:48.472
three hundred and twenty three hourly
absolute errors improve and one thousand

00:02:48.472 --> 00:02:49.932
and thirty four worsen.

00:02:50.562 --> 00:02:52.992
The model knows a typical level for a key,

00:02:53.222 --> 00:02:55.812
but cannot distinguish
all days sharing that key.

00:02:56.222 --> 00:02:59.412
We have not established why
this particular hour was quiet.

00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:04.393
There is one more result
to interpret carefully.

00:03:05.043 --> 00:03:08.316
The number of underpredicted
hours rises from two thousand,

00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:12.653
three hundred and ninety one to three
thousand, one hundred and forty three.

00:03:13.503 --> 00:03:18.696
Their mean deficit falls from about two
hundred and five point six zero to one

00:03:18.696 --> 00:03:20.613
hundred and thirteen point six one.

00:03:21.483 --> 00:03:25.293
Mean signed error remains negative
seventy two point three four,

00:03:25.723 --> 00:03:27.633
slightly more negative than before.

00:03:28.643 --> 00:03:34.323
Removing large quiet hour overpredictions
can improve absolute error without fixing

00:03:34.323 --> 00:03:36.693
the later period's overall level.

00:03:37.163 --> 00:03:41.253
Error size, frequency and direction
answer different questions.

00:03:42.143 --> 00:03:44.033
The implementation is ready for review.

00:03:44.603 --> 00:03:47.113
These results motivate the
next modelling discussion,

00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:50.393
without starting a later phase
or claiming learner mastery.

